#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151817 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa42f9bc-e3ad-56fa-8d82-4882e95ef826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Disjoint Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "841e5dcb-c092-5159-bfc4-d6580999c742" :question/math-academy-id "e-12760" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 2n \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}, \\qquad\nB = \\left\\{  2^{-n} \\mid n \\in \\mathbb {N}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A = \\{ 2, 4, 6,\\ldots \\}$\n\n- $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$\n\n- $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={2n∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={2(1),2(2),2(3),…} \\\\  & ={2,4,6,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={{2}^{-n}∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={{2}^{-1},{2}^{-2},{2}^{-3},…} \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is \n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\{ 2, 4, 6, \\ldots \\} \\cap \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{1}{8},\\ldots \\right\\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151818 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152102 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7079378e-2cec-550d-a647-405a0acb9b61" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Disjoint Union of Non-Overlapping Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "ea87c6f2-d0ef-5f8d-8f87-b9219272cedd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12761" :question/problem "Find the disjoint union of the sets $\\{ -1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\}$ and $\\{ v, w \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{ -1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\} \\cap \\{ v, w \\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{-1,1,\\sqrt{5}}⊔{v,w} & ={-1,1,\\sqrt{5}}\\cup {v,w} \\\\  & ={-1,1,\\sqrt{5},v,w}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151819 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152103 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5188a6-60a0-5e38-9164-201c3eb60cd5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Disjoint Union of Overlapping Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "65852684-d9ce-5830-b95f-9abae3dadd4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12762" :question/problem "Find the disjoint union of the sets $A_1 = \\{ 7,8 \\}$ and $A_2 = \\{ 2,4,8 \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the sets are overlapping since\n\n$$\n\\{ 7,8 \\} \\cap \\{ 2,4,8 \\} = \\{ 8 \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nSo, for each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is tagged with the corresponding index, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}={(7,1),(8,1)} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}={(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & ={7,8}⊔{2,4,8} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & ={(7,1),(8,1)}\\cup {(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)} \\\\  & ={(7,1),(8,1),(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa42f9bc-e3ad-56fa-8d82-4882e95ef826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Disjoint Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbad822-a55d-54b1-a8b2-602b7b51aef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374263} {:answer/id #uuid "dc63d538-6e7c-5e27-9165-dbb714b36dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374264} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0481fd-7e22-5729-91eb-6ecc92cb4c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374265} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0f06fd-3f41-512e-965b-e039b6730b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374266} {:answer/id #uuid "8c66ca44-6ad4-5d8b-bfec-2c566b8ead80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa0f06fd-3f41-512e-965b-e039b6730b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374266} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce7b2aa3-4c09-5d92-b3a7-10836c641729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0311da7-2e05-528f-8234-394bbb366e6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-174352" :question/problem "Which of the following pairs of sets are disjoint?\n\n- ${3,4,5}$ and ${6,7,8}$\n- ${-4,-3,-2}$ and ${2,3,4}$\n- $[-3,0]$ and $[0,3]$" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Pair I is disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n{3,4,5}\\cap {6,7,8}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair II is also disjoint. Indeed, the intersection of the sets is empty:\n\n$$\n{-4,-3,-2}\\cap {2,3,4}=∅\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Pair III is *not* disjoint since the intersection of the sets is not empty:\n\n$$\n[-3,0]\\cap [0,3]={0}\\ne ∅\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa42f9bc-e3ad-56fa-8d82-4882e95ef826" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Disjoint Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a75bed52-3210-5186-b0a2-6327a4e867bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374291} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7624ee-b577-5c08-9483-211bef34566c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374292} {:answer/id #uuid "8288ced1-b65e-589b-ba15-21f457e8303f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374293} {:answer/id #uuid "c528435e-d916-5f10-9a2d-3a71249cff32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374294} {:answer/id #uuid "954659ac-50d9-5b84-8951-cbd90c01f9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7624ee-b577-5c08-9483-211bef34566c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374292} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c713fc4-0c22-59b0-977e-74b521261f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f863cc29-74d9-5551-a15b-2146bb98a0f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-174372" :question/problem "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA={5n∣n\\in Z},\\,B={-\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B={-1,-2,-3,…}$\n- $A\\cap B=∅$\n- $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA\\cap B=∅\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is false.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={-\\frac{1}{n}∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={-1,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3},…} \\\\  & \\ne {-1,-2,-3,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={5n∣n\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-10,-5,0,5,10,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection is \n\n$$\nA\\cap B={…,-10,-5,0,5,10,…}\\cap {-1,-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{3},…}=∅.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
